@page "/activity"
@implements IDisposable
@inject ActivityLog Log
@inject AreaSnapshotCache Cache
@inject LightingEngineHost Engine
@inject ILogger<Activity> Logger

@*
	The engine's recent decisions, newest first: what each room did, when, and — the reason this page exists —
	why it declined to do anything. The owner's question was "why didn't that light come on", and the answer is
	a lux reading beside the threshold it was compared against; that line is the point of the timeline, so
	ActivityView.Describe builds it and a test asserts it.

	The source is the same adaptive_lighting_area event stream the dashboard already receives. Nothing new is
	subscribed and no log file is read: AreaSnapshotCache keeps the newest report per room for the cards and
	ActivityLog keeps the last few hundred, in order, for this page. What that inherits is worth stating —
	the timeline begins when the add-on started, not when the house did, and it holds only what Home Assistant
	delivered.

	Unlike the dashboard, this page does not hide rooms the owner switched off. The cards answer "what is my
	house doing", where a switched-off room is not part of the answer; this answers "what happened", and a
	report quietly dropped would be indistinguishable from a room that never reported.

	Two filters sit above it and compose: a room, and the categories a report falls into. Background tasks
	start switched off, which is what makes a quiet log readable — and is also why the filters state their own
	count and carry a way back. Everything they hide is still held; hidden and absent are not the same, and a
	page that let them look the same would be lying in exactly the way this one exists to stop.
*@

<PageTitle>Activity &mdash; Adaptive lighting</PageTitle>

<h1>Activity</h1>
<p class="lede">
	What each room decided, newest first. Times come from the machine running adaptive lighting, which is
	not always the one you are reading this on.
</p>

@if (_entries.Count > 0)
{
	@*
		The two filters, together, above the timeline. They are shown as one block because they compose: the
		chips count what the chosen room did, so narrowing the room re-reads every chip. Neither reaches the
		log — both are applied to the list already read out of it.
	*@
	<div class="log-filters">
		<div class="log-bar">
			<label class="log-filter">
				<span class="log-filter-label">Room</span>
				<select class="cell wide" @bind="_room" @bind:after="ApplyFilter">
					<option value="@ActivityView.AllRooms">All rooms</option>
					@foreach (string room in ActivityView.Rooms(_entries))
					{
						<option value="@room">@room</option>
					}
				</select>
			</label>

			@*
				The one live control on the page. New reports are counted as they arrive but are not inserted:
				a timeline that grows under the person reading it moves the line they were on, and this page is
				read exactly when something has gone wrong and somebody is trying to hold two facts at once.
				So the count updates by itself and the list only ever changes when a hand asks it to.

				It counts arrivals, never arrivals-that-match. A report landing in a category somebody has
				switched off must not raise a number they can then hunt for and never find.
			*@
			@if (Pending > 0)
			{
				<button type="button" class="log-new" @onclick="ShowNew">@PendingWord</button>
			}
		</div>

		@*
			Chips rather than a list of checkboxes behind a control: every category is readable without opening
			anything, and switching one is one tap. Each carries its own count within the chosen room, so a
			category with nothing in it says so rather than leading to a blank page. The tick is a filled square
			against a hollow one — shape as well as brass, so the state survives greyscale and a bright screen.
		*@
		<div class="log-cats" role="group" aria-label="Show these categories">
			<span class="log-filter-label">Show</span>

			@foreach (ActivityFilterChip chip in _chips)
			{
				<button type="button"
						class="@ChipClass(chip)"
						aria-pressed="@(chip.IsOn ? "true" : "false")"
						title="@chip.Title"
						@onclick="() => Toggle(chip.Category)">
					<span class="log-cat-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					<span class="log-cat-name">@chip.Label</span>
					<span class="log-cat-count">@chip.Count</span>
				</button>
			}
		</div>

		@*
			A filtered page that looks like an empty one is the same failure as a report the log lost, so the
			filters say what they are holding back and offer the single tap out of it. Not said here when the
			filters hid everything: the panel below then carries the same sentence at full size, and two
			identical ways out one above the other read as two different ones.
		*@
		@if (_visible.Count > 0 && HiddenNote is { Length: > 0 } hidden)
		{
			<p class="log-hidden">
				<span>@hidden</span>
				<button type="button" class="log-reset" @onclick="ShowEverything">Show everything</button>
			</p>
		}
	</div>
}

@if (_entries.Count == 0)
{
	@*
		Nothing recorded — which is a normal state, not a fault, and has to say which normal state it is. A
		house that has just been set up has every room switched off by design, and a quiet house genuinely can
		go a long time without a single transition. Left as a blank panel, all three of those read as broken
		software.
	*@
	<div class="empty">
		@if (!Engine.IsAttached)
		{
			<strong>Waiting for Home Assistant.</strong>
			<p>Reports reach this page through Home Assistant, so nothing can be recorded until the connection is up.</p>
			<p>This is normal for a minute after a restart.</p>
		}
		else if (!Engine.IsRunning)
		{
			<strong>Connected, but adaptive lighting isn't running.</strong>
			<p>@(Engine.Fault ?? "No reason was recorded.")</p>
			<p>See <a href="config">Configuration</a> for details.</p>
		}
		else
		{
			<strong>Nothing has happened yet.</strong>
			<p>
				Recording starts when adaptive lighting starts, and a room appears here the first time it
				decides something &mdash; movement, a change of house mode, or the light outside crossing the
				level it counts as dark.
			</p>
			<p>
				A quiet house can sit here for a long time. Rooms also start switched off after set-up, so if
				you have not turned any on yet there is nothing for the engine to decide &mdash;
				<a href="config?section=areas">choose the rooms it should look after</a>.
			</p>
		}
	</div>
}
else if (_inRoom.Count == 0)
{
	@* The room has nothing left in the buffer at all — a different quiet from the categories hiding it. *@
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>Nothing from @_room is still being held.</strong>
		<p>Its older reports may have been dropped to make room. The rest of the house is still here.</p>
		<p><button type="button" class="log-reset" @onclick="ShowEverything">Show everything</button></p>
	</div>
}
else if (_visible.Count == 0)
{
	@*
		The fourth quiet, and the only one that is the reader's own doing. It must not borrow the wording of
		"nothing has happened yet": reports are being held, they are only being held back, and the difference
		is one tap wide.
	*@
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>Every report is in a category you switched off.</strong>
		<p>
			@(_inRoom.Count == 1
				? "1 report is being held"
				: $"{_inRoom.Count} reports are being held")@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(_room) ? "" : $" from {_room}"),
			and none of them falls into the categories that are switched on.
		</p>
		<p><button type="button" class="log-reset" @onclick="ShowEverything">Show everything</button></p>
	</div>
}
else
{
	@* "Today" is read against the clock at render time, not at page load: a page left open past midnight
	   must not go on heading yesterday's entries Today. *@
	@foreach (ActivityDay day in ActivityView.GroupByDay(_visible, DateTimeOffset.Now))
	{
		<div class="floor-head">
			<span class="floor-name">@day.Heading</span>
		</div>

		<ol class="log">
			@foreach (ActivityEntry entry in day.Entries)
			{
				ActivityLine line = ActivityView.Describe(entry.Snapshot);

				<li class="log-row family-@AreaView.Family(entry.Snapshot.State)" @key="entry.Sequence">
					<div class="log-when">
						<time class="log-time" datetime="@entry.At.ToString("o", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
							  title="@Full(entry.At)">@Clock(entry.At)</time>
						<span class="log-room">@entry.AreaName</span>
					</div>
					<div class="log-body">
						<span class="log-what">@line.What</span>
						@if (line.Why is { Length: > 0 } why)
						{
							<span class="log-why">@why</span>
						}
					</div>
				</li>
			}
		</ol>
	}

	<p class="log-foot">@FootLine</p>
}

@code {
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> _entries = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> _inRoom = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> _visible = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityFilterChip> _chips = [];
	private string _room = ActivityView.AllRooms;
	private ActivityCategory _categories = ActivityView.DefaultCategories;
	private long _shownThrough;
	private IDisposable? _subscription;

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		ShowNew();

		// Sampled, and deliberately not used to re-read the log: a burst of transitions must not cost a burst
		// of renders, and the render it does cause only moves the "new reports" count. Blazor's diff leaves
		// every row already on the page untouched, so nothing shifts while somebody is reading it.
		_subscription = Cache.Changes
			.Sample(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1))
			.SubscribeSafe(_ => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged), Logger);
	}

	/// <summary>How many reports have arrived since the timeline was last drawn.</summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     A subtraction of sequence numbers rather than a count of entries, so it stays right once the oldest
	///     entries have been evicted: the buffer forgets, the counter does not.
	/// </remarks>
	private long Pending => Log.Newest - _shownThrough;

	private string PendingWord => Pending == 1 ? "1 new report — show it" : $"{Pending} new reports — show them";

	/// <summary>
	///     Draws everything the log is holding and moves the read mark to the newest report.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     One <see cref="ActivityLog.Read"/>, never the two properties in turn: a report landing between two
	///     separately-locked reads would be marked as shown while being absent from the list that was shown, and
	///     nothing would offer it again until the next report happened to arrive.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ShowNew()
	{
		ActivityTimeline timeline = Log.Read();

		_entries = timeline.Entries;
		_shownThrough = timeline.Newest;

		ApplyFilter();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Both filters, applied to what is already drawn rather than by re-reading the log, so changing one
	///     shows a narrower slice of the same timeline and not a silently refreshed one.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The room narrows first and the categories narrow what it left, which is also the order the two are
	///     counted in: the chips describe the chosen room, so somebody looking at one room sees what that room
	///     has done rather than what the house has.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ApplyFilter()
	{
		_inRoom = ActivityView.InRoom(_entries, _room);
		_visible = ActivityView.InCategories(_inRoom, _categories);
		_chips = ActivityView.Chips(_inRoom, _categories);
	}

	/// <summary>A chip's classes: whether it is letting rows through, and whether it has any to let through.</summary>
	private static string ChipClass(ActivityFilterChip chip) =>
		$"log-cat{(chip.IsOn ? " on" : string.Empty)}{(chip.Count == 0 ? " empty" : string.Empty)}";

	/// <summary>Switches one category on or off, leaving every other choice — including the room — alone.</summary>
	private void Toggle(ActivityCategory category)
	{
		_categories ^= category;
		ApplyFilter();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     The one tap back to the whole timeline: every category on, every room shown.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     It deliberately does not re-read the log. Somebody pressing this is asking to see what is being held
	///     back from them, and quietly folding in reports that arrived since would change two things at once —
	///     the "new reports" button stays the only thing that moves the list.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ShowEverything()
	{
		_room = ActivityView.AllRooms;
		_categories = ActivityView.AllCategories;
		ApplyFilter();
	}

	/// <summary>What the filters are keeping off the page, or <c>null</c> when they are keeping nothing off it.</summary>
	private string? HiddenNote => ActivityView.HiddenNote(_entries.Count, _visible.Count, _room, _categories);

	/// <summary>
	///     What the page is holding, and the cap it is held to. Said out loud because the alternative is a reader
	///     concluding that nothing happened before the oldest line, when in fact the oldest lines were dropped.
	/// </summary>
	private string FootLine
	{
		get
		{
			string held = _entries.Count == 1 ? "1 report" : $"{_entries.Count} reports";
			string shown = _visible.Count == _entries.Count ? held : $"{_visible.Count} of {held}";

			return _entries.Count < ActivityLog.Capacity
				? $"Showing {shown} since adaptive lighting started."
				: $"Showing {shown}. Only the most recent {ActivityLog.Capacity} are kept; older ones have been dropped.";
		}
	}

	private static string Clock(DateTimeOffset at) =>
		at.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

	private static string Full(DateTimeOffset at) =>
		at.ToLocalTime().ToString("dddd d MMMM yyyy, HH:mm:ss", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	public void Dispose() => _subscription?.Dispose();
}
